# nginx allocates two things once and lets the process exit take them back,
# so valgrind calls them lost.  Neither comes from this module: both traces
# end in nginx's own code, and the module does not appear in either.
#
# These two are the only entries, and that is measured, not assumed.  With
# an empty file the suite reports exactly these and nothing else -- no
# invalid read, no uninitialised value, no conditional jump.  Anything that
# was in here beyond them suppressed something that never happens.
#
# Note which cycle function they come through: Test::Nginx runs nginx with
# master_process off, so everything here is the single-process path.  An
# entry for the master's own path would never be reached from this suite
# and so would be an entry nobody can check.
#
# If a later nginx brings a finding back, valgrind will say so with a full
# trace.  Add it then, with a name and a reason, rather than keeping one
# here on the chance that it is still needed.

{
   nginx keeps the environment array until the process ends
   Memcheck:Leak
   match-leak-kinds: definite
   fun:malloc
   fun:ngx_alloc
   fun:ngx_set_environment
   ...
}

{
   nginx keeps the connection and event arrays until the process ends
   Memcheck:Leak
   match-leak-kinds: definite,indirect
   fun:malloc
   fun:ngx_alloc
   fun:ngx_event_process_init
   ...
}
